<!--Post: 3290--><!--Time: 1104741909-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3290"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Whoisbtk<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Whoisbtk<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, January 03, 2005 - 12:45 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Im re-posting this and one other to form a hypothetical reconstruction based on Charlie Otero&#39;s statements that his father knew of a threat days before. <BR> <BR> <BR>First Posted on Wednesday, December 29, 2004 - 11:23 am:      <BR> <BR>On the issue of BTK as a car thief, consider this:  <BR>1. He probably drove to some of the scenes. If he did then  <BR>2. He wouldn&#39;t park his car at or near the scene while he is killing, people might remember the tag/description, but he has to park it somewhere.  <BR>3. Park too far away and there&#39;s no point in driving. Park too close and it might be recognized.  <BR>4. When he&#39;s finished, lingering in the area is dangerous; he would leave as quickly as possible, and would want to conceal his appearance while doing so.  <BR>5. The Otero car driving away from the Otero house is not unusual, the car itself would provide some concealment.  <BR> <BR>My theory is that driving the victim&#39;s car away from the victim&#39;s house was the fastest and most inconspicous way to flee. The location of the victim&#39;s car would be close to the location of BTK&#39;s car. So he parks the victim&#39;s car and walks to his. If this is half a mile from the scene, who would connect someone walking to a car with a murder half a mile away?  <BR> <BR>Driving the victim&#39;s car away has always made more sense as a getaway than as a theft for theft&#39;s sake.  <BR> <BR>That said, I like your idea of revisiting the &#34;old&#34; BTK questions. Maybe the common answers and analysis are correct, maybe not.  <BR> <BR>How about re-examining whether he knew the Oteros? <BR> <BR> <BR><a href="mailto:whoisbtk@yahoo.com">whoisbtk@yahoo.com</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3290-->
<!--Post: 3291--><!--Time: 1104743307-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3291"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Whoisbtk<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Whoisbtk<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, January 03, 2005 - 1:08 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Posted on Saturday, January 01, 2005 - 9:16 pm:      <BR> <BR>-------------------------------------------------- ------------------------------ <BR>Mike_Kelleher: I hadn&#39;t thought through my suggestion well. You&#39;re correct. But what about changing your assumption on the accuracy of what C.O. said. Does that affect your profile?  <BR> <BR>Do we have to know absolutely whether Charlie Otero is accurate before we can talk about how it changes this case if he is?  <BR> <BR> <BR>I re-thought the Otero killing assuming C.O.&#39;s statement was true. The results are interesting enough that I&#39;m posting them even though they are pure speculation. I&#39;m using a hypothetical family, the Osbornes to be sure that this is taken as speculation only.  <BR> <BR>Let&#39;s look at the Osborne killing and assuming that BTK knew Mr. Osborne and vice-versa. Then let&#39;s assume Sonny Osborne is telling the truth about his father&#39;s fear that someone was trying to harm them.  <BR> <BR>If he was fearful enough to hide his children in a closet why didn&#39;t he go to the police for protection, because it&#39;s clear he didn&#39;t.  <BR> <BR>If Mr. Osborne didn&#39;t know of a threat, not calling the police makes perfect sense. But if he did know, not going to the police becomes incredibly significant.  <BR> <BR>One possibility is that the fallout from telling police was as bad or worse than dealing with the threat privately. If he goes to the police and says someone is trying to kill me they’ll ask me Who and Why.  <BR> <BR>At this point the real guessing begins. But suppose:  <BR> <BR>1. BTK and Mr. Osborne were selling drugs and BTK was Osborne’s source. Osborne gets a large quantity “fronted”, but Osborne is ripped off by his purchaser and can’t pay BTK for the drugs. BTK threatens to kill Osborne unless he pays.  <BR>2. If Osborne convinces police he needs protection, he has confessed to a drug conspiracy and goes to jail.  <BR> <BR>[This scenario is only an example, any significant joint criminal enterprise works]  <BR> <BR>WHILE THIS IS PURE GUESSING it does explain some of the curious aspects of the Osborne crime:  <BR>1. Why Osborne made no call to the police for help.  <BR>2. Why Osborne feared for his/his family’s safety  <BR>3. Why the children were killed: they could identify BTK as their parents killer in ways that people who didn’t know BTK couldn’t, such as: “he lived next door to us last year” or ‘he works with my father” or “ My father called him “John Doe” and said he knew him from the war.  <BR>4. Why entry was made while Mr. Osborne was taking children to school. It’s easier to subdue Mrs. Osborne and the two children without Mr. Osborne around, and then BTK has hostages to keep Mr. Osborne compliant when he comes back. [this also would explains why there was evidence of a fight between Mrs. Osborne and BTK, but not with Mr. Osborne]  <BR>5. It makes some sense of the “Coleman connection” If Mr. Osborne tells his wife, she tells something to friends at work, or BTK thinks she did. Kathryn Bright is killed because she &#42;might&#42; have talked to Mrs. Osborne.  <BR> <BR>This is purely hypothetical, but if Charlie Otero is correct, we might look in this direction.  <BR> <BR><a href="mailto:whoisbtk@yahoo.com">whoisbtk@yahoo.com</a>  <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3291-->
<!--Post: 3292--><!--Time: 1104743425-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3292"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Whoisbtk<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Whoisbtk<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, January 03, 2005 - 1:10 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Posted on Monday, January 03, 2005 - 12:15 am:      <BR> <BR>-------------------------------------------------- ------------------------------ <BR>Mike_Kelleher  <BR>J  <BR>I don&#39;t like drug dealing as the underlying issue either. I think your idea of prior military related homicides is much better. But I think I think some issue/reason has to be present in some form because:  <BR> <BR>&#42;IF&#42; Charlie is accurate about the fear, closet, etc.  <BR> <BR>&#42;THEN&#42; there is a reason BTK threatens Mr. Osborne, and  <BR> <BR>&#42;THEN&#42; there is a reason why Mr. Osborne won&#39;t ask police or adult friends for help.  <BR> <BR>We don&#39;t have to know what these reasons were for this hypothesis to function, but clearly this area of the hypothesis needs firming up.  <BR> <BR>If anyone has suggestions about what these prior crime/event/reasons might be, please jump in.  <BR> <BR> <BR>Cyndi: Those are good questions. My thinking on your points, plus one more:  <BR> <BR>Here’s One More: We have assumed that BTK knew two of the five children remained at home, what if he didn’t. He was surveilling the house most likely from a distance, the routine was Mr. Osborne taking all the children, BTK would have seen the children and Mr.Osborne get in and leave. If he wasn’t counting, he might think they were following their routine and all five children were gone.  <BR>He doesn’t think anyone is there but Mrs. Osborne until he pushes his way in.  <BR> <BR> <BR>1. Why didn&#39;t he wait for the other children to come home? Because two children had gone to school, and lunchtime becomes a problem because:  <BR> <BR>a. Photos show considerable snow, if the children at school are expecting to come home for lunch, and there&#39;s no ride, they call and there&#39;s no answer &#40;I don&#39;t know what it sounds like to call a phone that’s been cut&#41; but someone at school could report trouble on the line and repairmen show up. A child at school with a missing ride can be an issue.  <BR> <BR>b. BTK could not be sure the children wouldn&#39;t get a ride home at noon from a teacher or another parent, or that they wouldn&#39;t come home with a friend for lunch. Same for after school.  <BR> <BR>c. One or all three might stay after school, or go to a friend’s house, etc.  <BR> <BR>This could explain why he didn&#39;t wait for the remaining children: it&#39;s too unpredictable in terms of how long he waits, what if they get a ride from a concerned teacher who waits in a their car in the driveway for an &#34;OK&#34;.  <BR> <BR>[This would also set a time frame on the killing: he had to be done long enough before noon that he&#39;s gone before there&#39;s any risk of the children coming home for lunch. This fits the reported time of death as “sometime before noon” ”<a href="http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/news/special_pack" target=_top>http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/news/special_pack</a> ages/btk/history/8312877.htm]  <BR> <BR>2. Why didn&#39;t the surviving children identify BTK, or put another way, if the two children in the house knew who BTK was why didn&#39;t the surviving children know also?  <BR> <BR>a. All the children may have known who he was, but only the two at home have the specialized knowledge that he is the killer.  <BR> <BR>b. The two children who went to school lack this specialized knowledge. They might have known him, but not being there they could not link him to the killing of their parents. At best they could only identify him as one of hundreds of people their parents knew. This explains why the two at home are killed as witnesses, even though their siblings couldn&#39;t identify the killer  <BR> <BR>c. Immediately afterwards the children were taken into protective custody by SRS &#40;a child welfare agency&#41; and ultimately placed with family friends in New Mexico. He couldn&#39;t find them.  <BR> <BR>d. Also it would have soon been obvious to BTK that neither surviving child had given the police a &#34;smoking gun&#34; suspect, and the need to pursue them would diminish.  <BR> <BR>e. If the secret relationship between BTK and Mr. Osborne were so awful that he couldn&#39;t tell police, it wouldn&#39;t be unusual for a father to have concealed this kind of secret from his children.  <BR> <BR> <BR>Skoal and J  <BR> <BR>Thanks for the questions and critique [I tend to fall in love with my own theories; reality checks are always welcome]  <BR> <BR>Good points on the MO. Is it possible, though, that BTK may have combined business and pleasure; in other words, if he was going to kill anyway enjoying the act is a bonus.  <BR> <BR>Or, he may have learned to like it in this event, he may have been unaware of his tendencies until this killing, Mike_Kelleher could speak to that better.  <BR> <BR>Again please don’t get hung up on the drug issue, there’s no evidence of &#42;any&#42; criminal activity by any Otero. All I’m saying is that Charlie Otero’s statement raises two &#42; red flags&#42;: 1&#41; why did BTK want to hurt the Otero’s, and 2&#41; knowing of the threat why didn’t Mr. Otero call police?  <BR> <BR> <BR>I&#39;m proposing this hypothesis to test the assumption that C.O. was accurately reporting his father&#39;s awareness of a serious threat to his family rather than dismissing it out-of-hand because it doesn&#39;t fit a given theory of profile. Any plausible theory/profile must account for &#42;all&#42; of the evidence, not just some of it.  <BR> <BR>I remain concerned about a procrustean analytical process that rejects facts and evidence because they don&#39;t fit someone&#39;s theory, “Procruste’s Bed” &#40;<a href="http://www.bartleby.com/81/13673.html" target=_top>http://www.bartleby.com/81/13673.html</a>&#41;.  <BR> <BR> <BR><a href="mailto:whoisbtk@yahoo.com">whoisbtk@yahoo.com</a>  <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3292-->
